THAT IS NOT WHAT I THOUGHT IT WAS.  DO YOU KNOW WHAT I THOUGHT IT WAS?
@
message WIZARD_CONFIRMED
OH DEAR, YOU REALLY *ARE* A WIZARD!  SORRY TO HAVE BOTHERED YOU . . .
@
message WIZARD_IMPOSTOR
FOO, YOU ARE NOTHING BUT A CHARLATAN!
@
message START_TOO_SOON_HARD
EVEN WIZARDS HAVE TO WAIT LONGER THAN THAT!
@
message START_PRIME_CLOSED
I'M TERRIBLY SORRY, BUT COLOSSAL CAVE IS CLOSED.  OUR HOURS ARE:
@
message START_WIZARDS_ONLY
ONLY WIZARDS ARE PERMITTED WITHIN THE CAVE RIGHT NOW.
@
message START_DEMO_OFFER
WE DO ALLOW VISITORS TO MAKE SHORT EXPLORATIONS DURING OUR OFF HOURS.\n
WOULD YOU LIKE TO DO THAT?
@
message START_VERY_WELL
VERY WELL.
@
message DEMO_TIMEOUT
A LARGE CLOUD OF GREEN SMOKE APPEARS IN FRONT OF YOU.\n
IT CLEARS AWAY TO REVEAL A TALL WIZARD, CLOTHED IN GREY.\n
HE FIXES YOU WITH A STEELY GLARE AND DECLARES,\n
"THIS ADVENTURE HAS LASTED TOO LONG." \n
WITH THAT HE MAKES A SINGLE PASS OVER YOU WITH HIS HANDS,\n
AND EVERYTHING AROUND YOU FADES AWAY INTO A GREY NOTHINGNESS.
@
message DEMO_NO_SUSPEND
THERE'S NO POINT IN SUSPENDING A DEMONSTRATION GAME.
@
message START_SOON_WIZARD_ONLY
ONLY A WIZARD MAY CONTINUE AN ADVENTURE THIS SOON.
@
message START_RESUME_LATER
I SUGGEST YOU RESUME YOUR ADVENTURE AT A LATER TIME.
@
message HOURS_HEADER
COLOSSAL CAVE IS OPEN TO REGULAR ADVENTURERS AT THE FOLLOWING HOURS:
@
message MAINT_SEE_HOURS
DO YOU WISH TO SEE THE HOURS?
@
message MAINT_CHANGE_HOURS
DO YOU WISH TO CHANGE THE HOURS?
@
message MAINT_NEW_MAGIC
NEW MAGIC WORD (NULL TO LEAVE UNCHANGED):
@
message MAINT_NEW_MAGNM
NEW MAGIC NUMBER (NULL TO LEAVE UNCHANGED):
@
message MAINT_CHANGE_MOTD
DO YOU WISH TO CHANGE THE MESSAGE OF THE DAY?
@
message MAINT_OKAY_SAVE
OKAY.  YOU CAN SAVE THIS VERSION NOW.
@
message MAINT_NEWHRS_INTRO
NEW HOURS SPECIFIED BY DEFINING "PRIME TIME".\n
GIVE ONLY THE HOUR (E.G. 14, NOT 14:00 OR 2PM).\n
ENTER A NEGATIVE NUMBER AFTER LAST PAIR.
@
message MAINT_NEWHRS_DONE
NEW HOURS FOR COLOSSAL CAVE:
@
message MAINT_MOTD_LIMIT
LIMIT LINES TO 70 CHARS.  END WITH NULL LINE.
@
message MAINT_MOTD_TOO_LONG
LINE TOO LONG, RETYPE:
@
message MAINT_MOTD_NO_ROOM
NOT ENOUGH ROOM FOR ANOTHER LINE.  ENDING MESSAGE HERE.
@
message MAINT_RESCHEDULE_HOLIDAY
DO YOU WISH TO (RE)SCHEDULE THE NEXT HOLIDAY?
@
message MAINT_HOLIDAY_BEGIN
TO BEGIN HOW MANY DAYS FROM TODAY?
@
message MAINT_HOLIDAY_LAST
TO LAST HOW MANY DAYS (ZERO IF NO HOLIDAY)?
@
message MAINT_HOLIDAY_NAME
TO BE CALLED WHAT (UP TO 20 CHARACTERS)?
@
message MAINT_LATNCY_FLOOR
TOO SMALL!  ASSUMING MINIMUM VALUE (45 MINUTES).
@
message VENDING_HERE
THERE IS A MASSIVE VENDING MACHINE HERE.\n
THE INSTRUCTIONS ON IT READ:\n
"DROP COINS HERE TO RECEIVE FRESH BATTERIES."
@
message BATTERIES_HERE
THERE ARE FRESH BATTERIES HERE.
@
message BATTERIES_DISCARDED
SOME WORN-OUT BATTERIES HAVE BEEN DISCARDED NEARBY.
@
message INV_BATTERIES
BATTERIES
@
message LAMP_HINT_VENDING
YOUR LAMP IS GETTING DIM.\n
YOU'D BEST START WRAPPING THIS UP, UNLESS\n
YOU CAN FIND SOME FRESH BATTERIES.\n
I SEEM TO RECALL THERE'S A VENDING MACHINE IN THE MAZE.\n
BRING SOME COINS WITH YOU.
@
message LAMP_GO_BACK
YOUR LAMP IS GETTING DIM.\n
YOU'D BEST GO BACK FOR THOSE BATTERIES.
@
message LAMP_REPLACED
YOUR LAMP IS GETTING DIM.\n
I'M TAKING THE LIBERTY OF REPLACING THE BATTERIES.
@

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# TASK-FR8 additions (S1, 02-Sep-26). Text restored from adven.dat for
# messages that were absent from this corpus. Blocks below are NOT YET
# REFERENCED by adventure.am1 unless noted -- the wiring belongs to the
# task that needs each one (see PendingTasks/SESSION-GROUPS.md):
#   S3 wants FR8_NO_PASS_GRATE, FR8_SLIT_TOO_NARROW,
#            FR8_HOLES_MAIN_PASSAGE, FR8_HOLES_CAVE_IN
#   S4 wants FR8_AXE_BOUNCES_DRAGON, FR8_DRAGON_LOOKS_NASTY,
#            FR8_AXE_MISSES_BEAR, HERE_AXE_BY_BEAR
#   S5 wants HERE_VASE_ON_PILLOW
#   S6 wants FR8_DRINK_STREAM, FR8_BOTTLE_NOW_EMPTY
# Names carry an FR8_ prefix only where an unprefixed name would read as
# already-wired game text; the five HERE_* prop texts follow the existing
# object-text naming instead, since that is what they are.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

# adven.dat section 6, msg 38 -- section-8 default for LIGHT/ON, EXTINGUISH/OFF
message FR8_NO_LIGHT_SOURCE
YOU HAVE NO SOURCE OF LIGHT.
@

# msg 50 -- the SESAME/ABRA/SHAZAM/HOCUS family (special 3050)
message FR8_MAGIC_WORD_WORN_OUT
GOOD TRY, BUT THAT IS AN OLD WORN-OUT MAGIC WORD.
@

# msg 56 -- the Bedquilt/Swiss Cheese random bounce
message FR8_HOLES_MAIN_PASSAGE
YOU HAVE CRAWLED AROUND IN SOME LITTLE HOLES AND WOUND UP BACK IN THE\n
MAIN PASSAGE.
@

# msg 73 -- DRINK from a stream
message FR8_DRINK_STREAM
YOU HAVE TAKEN A DRINK FROM THE STREAM.  THE WATER TASTES STRONGLY OF\n
MINERALS, BUT IS NOT UNPLEASANT.  IT IS EXTREMELY COLD.
@

# msg 74 -- DRINK from the bottle
message FR8_BOTTLE_NOW_EMPTY
THE BOTTLE OF WATER IS NOW EMPTY.
@

# msg 93 -- DOWN at the grate (LOC 8) / UP below it (LOC 9), while locked
message FR8_NO_PASS_GRATE
YOU CAN'T GO THROUGH A LOCKED STEEL GRATE!
@

# msg 95 -- DOWN at the slit (LOC 7) and at the bottom of the pit (LOC 38)
message FR8_SLIT_TOO_NARROW
YOU DON'T FIT THROUGH A TWO-INCH SLIT!
@

# msg 126 -- Witt's End, WEST
message FR8_HOLES_CAVE_IN
YOU HAVE CRAWLED AROUND IN SOME LITTLE HOLES AND FOUND YOUR WAY\n
BLOCKED BY A RECENT CAVE-IN.  YOU ARE NOW BACK IN THE MAIN PASSAGE.
@

# msg 137
message FR8_LEAVE_BIRD_ALONE
OH, LEAVE THE POOR UNHAPPY BIRD ALONE.
@

# msg 138
message FR8_WANT_IS_AROUND_HERE
I DARESAY WHATEVER YOU WANT IS AROUND HERE SOMEWHERE.
@

# msg 144 -- FILL VASE. The bottle variant is msg 106, already present
# and correctly used as FILL_NOTHING_HERE.
message FR8_FILL_VASE_NOTHING
THERE IS NOTHING HERE WITH WHICH TO FILL THE VASE.
@

# msg 145
message FR8_VASE_SHATTERED
THE SUDDEN CHANGE IN TEMPERATURE HAS DELICATELY SHATTERED THE VASE.
@

# msg 152 -- THROW AXE at a live dragon
message FR8_AXE_BOUNCES_DRAGON
THE AXE BOUNCES HARMLESSLY OFF THE DRAGON'S THICK SCALES.
@

# msg 153 -- travel past a live dragon (LOC 119 and LOC 121; LOC 120
# is the dragon-DEAD canyon and has no msg-153 row)
message FR8_DRAGON_LOOKS_NASTY
THE DRAGON LOOKS RATHER NASTY.  YOU'D BEST NOT TRY TO GET BY.
@

# msg 155 -- section-8 default for BRIEF. The port's WHAT is msg 61,
# a different message, correctly present.
message FR8_ON_WHAT
ON WHAT?
@

# msg 164 -- THROW AXE at a live bear
message FR8_AXE_MISSES_BEAR
THE AXE MISSES AND LANDS NEAR THE BEAR WHERE YOU CAN'T GET AT IT.
@

# msg 169
message FR8_BEAR_STILL_CHAINED
THE BEAR IS STILL CHAINED TO THE WALL.
@

# msg 170 -- TAKE CHAIN while the bear is still there. The port's
# CHAIN_LOCK_OK is msg 172, a different message, correctly present.
message FR8_CHAIN_STILL_LOCKED
THE CHAIN IS STILL LOCKED.
@

# msg 200 -- maintenance mode
message FR8_IS_THIS_ACCEPTABLE
IS THIS ACCEPTABLE?
@

# adven.dat section 12, msg 31 -- maintenance mode. Its companion msg 32
# is already present as CORE_IMAGE_REMINDER.
message FR8_BREAK_AND_SAVE_CORE
BREAK OUT OF THIS AND SAVE YOUR CORE-IMAGE.
@

# adven.dat section 5 prop texts. The object grammar carries one heremsg
# per object, so these prop-1 variants are reached by poking objHereMsg
# at runtime -- the poke belongs to the task that owns each mechanic.

# object 2 prop 1 -- a lit lamp on the floor
message HERE_LAMP_LIT
THERE IS A LAMP SHINING NEARBY.
@

# object 8 prop 1 -- the caged bird on the floor
message HERE_BIRD_IN_CAGE
THERE IS A LITTLE BIRD IN THE CAGE.
@

# object 28 prop 1 -- after THROW AXE at the bear (S4)
message HERE_AXE_BY_BEAR
THERE IS A LITTLE AXE LYING BESIDE THE BEAR.
@

# object 32 prop 1 -- after the troll bridge collapses
message HERE_CHASM_WRECKAGE
THE WRECKAGE OF A BRIDGE (AND A DEAD BEAR) CAN BE SEEN AT THE BOTTOM\n
OF THE CHASM.
@

# object 58 prop 1 -- the Ming vase set down on the velvet pillow (S5)
message HERE_VASE_ON_PILLOW
THE VASE IS NOW RESTING, DELICATELY, ON A VELVET PILLOW.
@

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# TASK-FR7 7a additions (S12, 03-Sep-26). Two adven.dat section-8
# defaults for the three action verbs the port was missing. Both texts
# already exist in this corpus under another message's name -- msg 110 is
# WAKE_DEFAULT (ACTSPK(WAKE) is also 110) and msg 14 is FEED_NOT_BEAR --
# and both are named again here rather than shared, which is what this
# corpus already does for msg 110's two roles (WAKE_DEFAULT and
# FEED_BEAR_DEAD). One record per role keeps a later edit to one verb's
# text from silently changing another's.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

# msg 110 -- TWO USES. adven.f4 9150's
# "IF(OBJ.NE.0.AND.OBJ.NE.WATER)SPK=110", and DOWN/JUMP at the volcano
# view (LOC 126, .dat row `126 610 30 39`). The name is the first use;
# TASK-FR2 step 3 added the travel rows against it rather than
# duplicating the text, because a message costs two words of bank 1 in
# the msg_* table.
message DRINK_NOT_WATER
DON'T BE RIDICULOUS!
@

# msg 14 -- ACTSPK(CALM), adven.dat section 8 row "10 14". CALM has no
# behaviour in the source at all: 4090 index 10 goes straight to 2011.
message CALM_DEFAULT
I'M GAME.\n
WOULD YOU CARE TO EXPLAIN HOW?
@

# msg 147 -- ACTSPK(FOO), adven.dat section 8 row "25 147". FEE/FIE/FOE/
# FOO/FUM with a noun is 4090 index 25, which is 2011 and nothing else,
# so the incantation does not advance (TASK-FR27 N13). Same text as
# SWIM_MSG and named separately for the same reason msg 110 carries
# three names in this file: one record per role.
message FOO_DEFAULT
I DON'T KNOW HOW.
@

# advn2.f4 133-135 (GETIN's FORMAT 21), not a section-6 message -- the
# tokenizer's own refusal of a third word, printed and then the line is
# read again with no turn taken (TASK-FR27 N10).
message STICK_TO_TWO_WORDS
PLEASE STICK TO 1- AND 2-WORD COMMANDS.
@

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Movement -- the direction names, numbered with adven.dat's OWN
# section-4 motion numbers (TASK-FR2, 06-Sep-26).
#
# They used to be the port's own 1..30 with TASK-FR2 step 1's 31..67
# appended, which meant every section-3 travel row had to be translated
# on the way in. The exit entry's direction field is seven bits now
# (TASK-FR2's 05-Sep-26 addendum), so the source's numbering fits and the
# rows transcribe instead: `1 2 2 44 29` names motions 2, 44 and 29, and
# those are ROAD, WEST and UP right here.
#
# The list is in adven.dat's numeric order. Gaps are real -- the numbers
# below that no direction claims are words the port handles elsewhere:
#     3  ENTER                   the ENTER WATER/STREAM transform
#     8  BACK/RETRE/RETUR        doBack
#    26  AWKWA                   no travel row ported yet (FR2 step 3)
#    36  LEFT                    still aliased to NE (FR2 step 4)
#    37  RIGHT                   still aliased to SE (FR2 step 4)
#    57  DESCR/EXAMI/LOOK/TOUCH  doLook
#    67  CAVE                    doCave
#    76  MAIN/OFFIC              no travel row ported yet (FR2 step 3)
#
# PASSAGE and TUNNEL are one adven.dat word (23, PASSA/TUNNE) under two
# spellings and deliberately share a code; nothing else may.
#
# XYZZY, PLUGH and PLOVE are numbered but have NO doMove verb row below,
# so those three words answer exactly as they did before FR2 step 1:
#   XYZZY/PLUGH/PLOVE -- doXyzzy/doPlugh/doPlove own these words and do
#     the teleport themselves. Their travel-row form is FR2's step 2/3
#     work, and moving them onto doMove before the rows exist would just
#     break three working magic words.
#
# A direction lives in three files -- here, advdataloader.c's dirTable,
# and adventure.am1's #define DIR_* block -- and the loader compares only
# the first two. adventure_fr2_motion_vocabulary_test.py compares all
# three. Trailing comments are NOT allowed on these rows: the lexer only
# honors a '#' in column 1.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
direction ROAD    0d2
# TASK-FR2 step 4 (06-Sep-26): the three de-aliased words.  ENTER is
# adven.dat motion 3 and its ONLY word (section 4 line "3 ENTER"); it
# was aliased onto DIR_IN, whose own word list is IN/INSID/INWAR
# (motion 19).  LEFT (36) and RIGHT (37) are likewise one word each and
# were aliased onto NE/SE.
direction ENTER   0d3
direction UPSTR   0d4
direction DOWNS   0d5
direction FORES   0d6
direction FORWARD 0d7
direction VALLE   0d9
direction STAIR   0d10
direction OUT     0d11
direction BUILD   0d12
direction GULLY   0d13
direction STREA   0d14
direction ROCK    0d15
direction BED     0d16
direction CRAWL   0d17
direction COBBL   0d18
direction IN      0d19
direction SURFA   0d20
direction NULL    0d21
direction DARK    0d22
direction PASSAGE 0d23
direction TUNNEL  0d23
direction LOW     0d24
direction CANYO   0d25
direction GIANT   0d27
direction VIEW    0d28
direction UP      0d29
direction DOWN    0d30
direction PIT     0d31
direction OUTDO   0d32
direction CRACK   0d33
direction STEPS   0d34
direction DOME    0d35
direction LEFT    0d36
direction RIGHT   0d37
direction HALL    0d38
direction JUMP    0d39
direction BARRE   0d40
direction OVER    0d41
direction ACROSS  0d42
direction EAST    0d43
direction WEST    0d44
direction NORTH   0d45
direction SOUTH   0d46
direction NE      0d47
direction SE      0d48
direction SW      0d49
direction NW      0d50
direction DEBRI   0d51
direction HOLE    0d52
direction WALL    0d53
direction BROKE   0d54
direction Y2      0d55
direction CLIMB   0d56
direction FLOOR   0d58
direction ROOM    0d59
direction SLIT    0d60
direction SLAB    0d61
direction XYZZY   0d62
direction DEPRE   0d63
direction ENTRA   0d64
direction PLUGH   0d65
direction SECRE   0d66
direction CROSS   0d69
direction BEDQU   0d70
direction PLOVE   0d71
direction ORIEN   0d72
direction CAVER   0d73
direction SHELL   0d74
direction RESER   0d75
direction FORK    0d77

flag DARK 0d131072
flag DWARF 0d65536
flag SURFACE 0d32768
flag PIRATE_FORBID 0d16384

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# The surface, LOC 1-10 (TASK-FR2 step 2, 04-Sep-26).  Every exit below
# is a transcription of an adven.dat section-3 row, not a translation:
# since the renumber a direction's DIR_* code IS its section-4
# motion number, so `1 2 2 44 29` reads off as ROAD/WEST/UP -> LOC 2.
# The rows are listed in the source's own order, grouped by the row they
# come from, with that row quoted beside the first exit of the group.
#
# Two adven.dat rows in this range are deliberately NOT table rows:
#   LOC 3's `3 11 62` (XYZZY) and `3 33 65` (PLUGH) -- doXyzzy/doPlugh
#     own those words and never fall through to the travel scan, so a
#     table row for them would be unreachable data.
#   LOC 10's NULL (21) in `10 9 11 20 21 43` -- adven.f4 1005 answers
#     NULL at label 8, ahead of the travel scan, by staying put, so the
#     player can never reach this row. FR27 N13 (05-Sep-26) gave NULL and
#     NOWHE vocabulary rows, but they name doNull, not doMove, so the row
#     is still unreachable -- deliberately, and for the source's reason.
# LOC 3's `3 79 5 14` is not a table row either: LOC 79 is forced motion
# with no way out but the force, so it is a bounce in adventure.am1
# (bldSewerBounce), the same shape as LOC 22 and LOC 32.
# TASK-FR26 N6 (05-Sep-26) made that the rule for every forced LOC: 16, 26,
# 40 and 59 used to be built as standing rooms and are now bounces too, so
# no forced LOC is a room in this corpus except LOC 21 (R_FISSURE_DEATH),
# which the player never reaches either -- dfdCheckRoom kills ahead of the
# scan, and the room survives only for dwMoveOne's FORCED exclusion.
#
# ENTER (motion 3) appears in LOC 1's, LOC 3's and LOC 8's rows and has
# no direction of its own -- this port aliases the word onto DIR_IN.
# That is right at LOC 1 and LOC 8, where ENTER and IN share a row, and
# wrong at LOC 3, where the source's ENTER goes outside and IN has no
# row at all.  Left alone here: giving ENTER its own code is vocabulary
# work of the same kind as FR2 step 4's LEFT/RIGHT, not step 2's.
# FR27 N7 landed 05-Sep-26 and narrowed what the alias still covers:
# tlEnterVerb now does adven.f4 19999's "ENTER <word> -> 2800", so only
# BARE ENTER reaches this row, and that is the case FR2 step 4 owns.
room R_ROAD
longmsg ROAD_LONG
shortmsg ROAD_SHORT
flag DARK no
flag DWARF no
flag SURFACE yes
flag PIRATE_FORBID no
# 1 2 2 44 29
exit ROAD  R_HILL
exit WEST  R_HILL
exit UP    R_HILL
# 1 3 3 12 19 43
# FR2 step 4 (06-Sep-26): ENTER is its own word now, so it needs its own
# entry here.  It worked before only because IN shares this row; that is
# what made the alias look right at this LOC.
exit BUILD R_BUILDING
exit ENTER R_BUILDING
exit IN    R_BUILDING
exit EAST  R_BUILDING
# 1 4 5 13 14 46 30
exit DOWNS R_VALLEY
exit GULLY R_VALLEY
exit STREA R_VALLEY
exit SOUTH R_VALLEY
exit DOWN  R_VALLEY
# 1 5 6 45 43      (43/EAST already matched the R_BUILDING row above)
exit FORES R_FOREST
exit NORTH R_FOREST
# 1 8 63
exit DEPRE R_GRATE
end
room R_BUILDING
longmsg BUILDING_LONG
shortmsg BUILDING_SHORT
flag DARK no
flag DWARF no
flag SURFACE yes
flag PIRATE_FORBID no
# 3 1 3 11 32 44
# FR2 step 4 (06-Sep-26): ENTER now wired.  This LOC is why the alias was
# a defect and not just an approximation -- the source sends ENTER OUTSIDE
# from in here, and this LOC has no IN row at all, so aliased ENTER found
# no exit and answered moveBlocked's IN/OUT class ("I DON'T KNOW IN FROM
# OUT HERE.").
exit OUT   R_ROAD
exit ENTER R_ROAD
exit OUTDO R_ROAD
exit WEST  R_ROAD
end
room R_HILL
longmsg HILL_LONG
shortmsg HILL_SHORT
flag DARK no
flag DWARF no
flag SURFACE yes
flag PIRATE_FORBID no
# 2 1 2 12 7 43 30
exit ROAD    R_ROAD
exit BUILD   R_ROAD
exit FORWARD R_ROAD
exit EAST    R_ROAD
exit DOWN    R_ROAD
# 2 5 6 45 46
exit FORES   R_FOREST
exit NORTH   R_FOREST
exit SOUTH   R_FOREST
end
room R_VALLEY
longmsg VALLEY_LONG
shortmsg VALLEY_SHORT
flag DARK no
flag DWARF no
flag SURFACE yes
flag PIRATE_FORBID no
# 4 1 4 12 45
exit UPSTR R_ROAD
exit BUILD R_ROAD
exit NORTH R_ROAD
# 4 5 6 43 44 29
exit FORES R_FOREST
exit EAST  R_FOREST
exit WEST  R_FOREST
exit UP    R_FOREST
# 4 7 5 46 30
exit DOWNS R_SLIT
exit SOUTH R_SLIT
exit DOWN  R_SLIT
# 4 8 63
exit DEPRE R_GRATE
end
# LOC 5.  The three-word 50% row and the LOC-6 row after it are one
# cascade, not two independent exits: adven.f4's label 12 falls a failed
# row through to the NEXT row with a different N field REGARDLESS of that
# row's own motion words, so FORWARD and NORTH reach LOC 6 as well even
# though `5 6 6` names only FORES.  The port's RAND row does the same
# thing by construction -- dmRand's miss goes to dmAdvance, which scans
# on for the next entry with this direction -- so each of the three words
# needs both halves written out.
#
# WEST and SOUTH are genuine self-loops (`5 5 44 46`); the audit's
# self-loop check compares against the original and passes them.  The
# port used to send SOUTH to the End of Road, which was an invented
# shortcut with no row behind it and is now gone.
room R_FOREST
longmsg FOREST_LONG
shortmsg FOREST_SHORT
flag DARK no
flag DWARF no
flag SURFACE yes
flag PIRATE_FORBID no
# 5 4 9 43 30
exit VALLE   R_VALLEY
exit EAST    R_VALLEY
exit DOWN    R_VALLEY
# 5 50005 6 7 45  -- 50% stay in the forest
# 5 6 6           -- and on a miss, the cascade lands at LOC 6
exit FORES   R_FOREST rand 0d50
exit FORES   R_FOREST2
exit FORWARD R_FOREST rand 0d50
exit FORWARD R_FOREST2
exit NORTH   R_FOREST rand 0d50
exit NORTH   R_FOREST2
# 5 5 44 46
exit WEST    R_FOREST
exit SOUTH   R_FOREST
end
room R_FOREST2
longmsg FOREST2_LONG
shortmsg FOREST2_SHORT
flag DARK no
flag DWARF no
flag SURFACE yes
flag PIRATE_FORBID no
# 6 1 2 45
exit ROAD  R_ROAD
exit NORTH R_ROAD
# 6 4 9 43 44 30
exit VALLE R_VALLEY
exit EAST  R_VALLEY
exit WEST  R_VALLEY
exit DOWN  R_VALLEY
# 6 5 6 46
exit FORES R_FOREST
exit SOUTH R_FOREST
end
room R_SLIT
longmsg SLIT_LONG
shortmsg SLIT_SHORT
flag DARK no
flag DWARF no
flag SURFACE yes
flag PIRATE_FORBID no
# 7 1 12
exit BUILD R_ROAD
# 7 4 4 45
exit UPSTR R_VALLEY
exit NORTH R_VALLEY
# 7 5 6 43 44
exit FORES R_FOREST
exit EAST  R_FOREST
exit WEST  R_FOREST
# 7 8 5 15 16 46
exit DOWNS R_GRATE
exit ROCK  R_GRATE
exit BED   R_GRATE
exit SOUTH R_GRATE
# 7 595 60 14 30   (595 -> message 95, print and stay)
# A message-only row prints and ends the turn here, as every other one in
# the port does; FR26 N3 has the source re-describing the room afterwards.
exit SLIT  none msg FR8_SLIT_TOO_NARROW
exit STREA none msg FR8_SLIT_TOO_NARROW
exit DOWN  none msg FR8_SLIT_TOO_NARROW
end
room R_GRATE
longmsg GRATE_LONG
shortmsg GRATE_SHORT
flag DARK no
flag DWARF no
flag SURFACE yes
flag PIRATE_FORBID no
# 8 5 6 43 44 46
exit FORES R_FOREST
exit EAST  R_FOREST
exit WEST  R_FOREST
exit SOUTH R_FOREST
# 8 1 12
exit BUILD R_ROAD
# 8 7 4 13 45
exit UPSTR R_SLIT
exit GULLY R_SLIT
exit NORTH R_SLIT
# 8 303009 3 19 30 / 8 593 3   (grate open -> LOC 9, else message 93)
# FR2 step 4 (06-Sep-26): ENTER gets its own entry -- it worked here only
# by sharing IN's row.  ENTER is on the message row `8 593 3` too, and
# adven.f4 label 12 falls to the next destination group WITHOUT re-testing
# the word, so all three words reach message 93 on a shut grate; the fused
# `msg` clause already expresses that for DOWN and IN.
